---
scope: src
intent: >
  The aidemd-mcp server exists so that any MCP-compatible agent, upon connecting
  to it, immediately knows how to read, write, and validate .aide specs without
  being taught. The tool list IS the teaching surface — the convention transfers
  at handshake time, not through CLAUDE.md, external docs, or user configuration.
  Success is a brand-new agent session in a brand-new project using AIDE
  correctly within its first few tool calls.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - An agent reading only the tool list learns what .aide files are, the four
      file types, the never-both-.aide-and-intent.aide naming rule, the
      next-to-orchestrators placement rule, and the read-specs-before-code
      workflow.
    - Five tools cover the full AIDE lifecycle with no overlap — discover (map),
      read (drill in), scaffold (create with naming enforcement), validate
      (health check), init (one-command bootstrap).
    - Behavior is identical across every supported MCP client (Claude Code,
      Cursor, Windsurf, Copilot, Zed). Client-specific logic is confined to
      aide_init; the core tools are client-agnostic.
    - aide_init turns a cold project AIDE-ready in one command and is safely
      re-runnable — methodology written to the host's config file, slash
      commands scaffolded for every pipeline phase, MCP server wired into the
      project's MCP config, IDE file associations configured.
    - Every tool description is complete enough to teach the convention on its
      own. No "see docs", no abbreviation, no assumed prior knowledge.
  undesired:
    - A tool description that points at an external document or says the agent
      should "read CLAUDE.md for details". The handshake is the only
      documentation the agent is guaranteed to see.
    - Divergent behavior between MCP clients on the same input. If Claude Code
      and Cursor see different results from `aide_discover`, the server has
      failed its client-parity invariant.
    - aide_init that is not idempotent — a project initialized twice with
      duplicated methodology blocks, duplicated slash commands, or a broken
      .mcp.json is a corrupted project, not an initialized one.
    - A sixth tool added to the surface because a responsibility "didn't quite
      fit" one of the five. Dilution of the teaching surface is a regression
      even when the new tool is technically correct.
    - Truncated descriptions justified by "token budget". Tokens spent teaching
      are recovered every time an agent doesn't have to fetch an external doc
      or silently violate the convention.
---

## Context

Every AI agent re-learns conventions from scratch at the start of every session.
Teaching AIDE through a CLAUDE.md snippet works in one project with one agent
framework, but it doesn't scale: each new project, each new framework, each new
contributor must rediscover the rules, and any of those copies can go stale.
aidemd-mcp moves the teaching into the MCP handshake itself, which is the only
documentation surface a compliant MCP client is required to present to the
model verbatim, in every session, in every project, without user action.

That protocol guarantee is the leverage point this module is built around. The
entire server exists to protect and use it.

## Strategy

**Treat the tool list as the primary teaching artifact, not as API reference.**
Each description introduces the concept it invokes before invoking it:
`aide_discover` explains what .aide files are and why you read them before
code; `aide_scaffold` explains the naming-conflict rule before it describes
its `type` enum. An agent that only ever reads the tool list must still come
away with the full convention. The cost of a longer description is recovered
every time an agent doesn't need to fetch an external doc, and dwarfed by the
cost of an agent silently violating the convention.

**Five tools, one per lifecycle phase, no more.** Discover is the map; read is
the drill; scaffold is the pen; validate is the health check; init is the
bootstrap. Adding a sixth tool requires showing that none of the five can
absorb the responsibility. Fewer tools means less surface for an agent to get
wrong and less description budget competing for attention at handshake time.

**Client parity via MCP, not per-client shims.** The server speaks standard
MCP over stdio. All client-specific branching lives in `aide_init`, which
writes config files for whichever framework it detects. The core four tools
are fed identical input schemas regardless of which client is talking, because
parity is what lets the teaching surface be reused across clients.

**Idempotency as an invariant, not a feature.** Init, scaffold, and validate
are all safe to re-run on a project already in that state. Agents retry tool
calls when uncertain; a non-idempotent call corrupts on the retry. This is
especially load-bearing for init, which writes files an agent should never
feel nervous about re-running.

## Good examples

A new agent connects to aidemd-mcp for the first time and, before touching a
single file, reasons:

> There are .aide spec files next to orchestrator code that I should read
> before I read the code. Four types exist: `.aide` is the default intent
> spec; `intent.aide` is the same thing renamed when a `research.aide` sits
> alongside; `research.aide` holds raw research; `todo.aide` is a QA queue.
> I must never see both `.aide` and `intent.aide` in the same folder. My
> workflow is: call `aide_discover` with no path for the project map, then
> drill into the relevant subtree with a path, then `aide_read` specific
> specs as needed.

Every piece of that reasoning came from the tool list alone. No CLAUDE.md was
read; no README was fetched. That is the target experience at handshake time.

A user runs `aide_init` on a fresh project and gets back a summary like:

> Detected framework: Claude Code.
> Created CLAUDE.md with AIDE methodology block.
> Created slash commands for research, spec, build, QA, and fix phases.
> Wired aidemd-mcp into .mcp.json.
> Configured .zed/settings.json to render .aide as Markdown.
> Skipped VS Code extension — `code` CLI not on PATH.

Running the same command a second time returns the same summary with
"already present" markers instead of "created", and nothing on disk changes.

## Bad examples

A tool description that reads "Scaffold new .aide files. See the AIDE docs for
naming rules." The agent has learned nothing about the naming-conflict rule
and will happily create a `.aide` next to an existing `research.aide`,
producing exactly the corruption the tool was built to prevent. The
description failed its only job.

A tool description that enumerates file types without explaining when each
applies — a bare list of ".aide, intent.aide, research.aide, todo.aide" with
no accompanying rule. Agents pattern-match on the names, guess, and get it
wrong. Teaching is not enumeration.

An `aide_init` that appends its methodology block to CLAUDE.md on every run,
so a project initialized three times has three stacked copies of the same
block. Idempotency was treated as a nice-to-have instead of an invariant, and
the file is now corrupted.

A sixth tool — say `aide_lint` — added to the surface because validation
rules "felt different" from health checks. Both tools show up in the
handshake, overlap in responsibility, and the agent has to guess which one to
call. The teaching surface has been diluted for no gain, because the
responsibility should have lived inside validate.
